@virchan 
thanks for the reply

here is a dummy example showing the behavior using only sklearn code.
increase the size of the data in the second cell to see the HTML navigation issue

```python
import marimo

__generated_with = "0.23.9"
app = marimo.App(width="full")


@app.cell
def _():
    from sklearn.base import ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin, TransformerMixin, BaseEstimator
    import numpy as np

    class SomeEstimator(ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin, TransformerMixin, BaseEstimator):
        def __sklearn_is_fitted__(self):
            return hasattr(self, "data_")

        def fit(self, x):
            self.data_ = x
            self._n_features_out = x.shape[0]
            return self

    return SomeEstimator, np


@app.cell
def _(SomeEstimator, np):
    se = SomeEstimator()

    data = np.ones((1000, 1))

    se.fit(data)
    se
    return


if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run()
```

I do confirm that the provided example code above fails with the current scikit-learn dev version. 

Maybe there is a way to avoid the `ValueError`.  For example adding a try/except when reading attribute by attribute.. (avoiding `getattr`) in [sklearn/base.py](https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/blob/07f42cca7580bd3b917860721e2e3344abca66e5/sklearn/base.py#L360)

@glemaitre @jeremiedbb for visibility: They would know better than me if a new feature is better.
